==Old English==

===Etymology===
{{proto|Germanic|aitra-|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|oid-|to swell|lang=ang}}. Cognate with Old Saxon [[ettor]], Old High German [[eitar]] (German [[Eiter]] ‘pus’), Old Norse [[eitr]] (Swedish [[etter]]). The Indo-European root was also the source of {{etyl|grc|-}} {{polytonic|[[οἶδος]]}} ‘swelling’, Slavic *{{Cyrl|jadъ}} (Russian {{l|ru|яд}} ‘poison’).

